"""Lore: the longer story of each weapon, shown when you examine it (play.examine)."""
LORE = {
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------ USA
    "m1_garand": "Patton called it the greatest battle implement ever devised, and the men who carried its nine and a half pounds across Europe and the Pacific mostly agreed. Eight rounds went in on an en-bloc clip, and a careless recruit soon learned why a bruised, bloody thumb was called 'M1 thumb'. When the last round went, the empty clip flew out with a bright ping that some men swore the enemy listened for.",
    "m1903": "Old sergeants loved the '03: a smooth bolt, fine sights, five rounds from a stripper clip and accuracy a marksman could brag about. Its action borrowed so much from Mauser that the United States paid the German firm royalties until 1917. The Marines carried it ashore on Guadalcanal in 1942, and long after the Garand arrived, rifle grenadiers and snipers held on to theirs.",
    "m1903a4": "Remington built it from the wartime M1903A3, fitted a commercial Weaver hunting scope and left off the iron sights altogether. The scope sat low over the action, so stripper clips would not fit and each round had to be pressed into the magazine by hand. Snipers in Normandy and the Pacific cursed the Weaver's low power and its habit of fogging in rain and jungle damp, but the rifle beneath it shot as well as any Springfield.",
    "m1_carbine": "Barely five and a half pounds, it was meant to replace the pistol for officers, radiomen, mortarmen and drivers, and it ended up in the hands of almost anyone who could get one. The little .30 Carbine round was mild and quick, good out to a couple of hundred yards and not much further. More than six million were built, more than any other American small arm of the war, some of them by firms that had made typewriters, jukeboxes and business machines.",
    "thompson_m1a1": "Stripped of its gangster-era frills - no finned barrel, no compensator, no drum - the M1A1 was built quickly and cheaply for a world war, at a fraction of the pre-war price. It still weighed over ten pounds empty, and its fat .45 slugs hit like a hammer at close quarters. Squad leaders, scouts and paratroopers carried it through Italy, France and the Pacific; beyond a hundred yards its slow, heavy bullets dropped away.",
    "thompson_1928": "The Thompson of 1940 was walnut and blued steel, with a finned barrel, a Cutts compensator and a Blish lock that did very little. Britain, desperate after Dunkirk, bought every one it could get, and when Churchill was photographed holding one that summer, German propaganda turned the picture into a gangster poster. The 50-round drum was heavy, rattled and slow to load, so most men settled for the stick magazine.",
    "m3_grease": "It looked exactly like the tool in the motor pool, and the men named it accordingly. Guide Lamp, a General Motors division that had made car headlamps, stamped it out of sheet steel for about twenty dollars apiece. It fired so slowly that a steady finger could squeeze off single shots, and tank crews liked it because it fitted inside a turret; the flimsy crank cocking handle gave way in the M3A1 to a finger hole in the bolt.",
    "bar": "John Browning's automatic rifle went to France in 1918, and between the wars Clyde Barrow stole them from National Guard armouries. The A2 fired automatic only, at a choice of two rates, from a 20-round magazine, and with no quick-change barrel the BAR man learned to shoot in short bursts. It weighed close to twenty pounds, many carriers threw away the bipod to save weight, and every rifle squad was built around it.",
    "m1919": "The air-cooled cousin of Browning's water-cooled M1917, the 'thirty-cal' went everywhere: on tanks, jeeps and half-tracks as well as on the infantry's low tripod. Gun and tripod together came to about forty-five pounds, and changing a hot barrel was a slow job done with an asbestos mitten, so gunners learned to fire in bursts. Its steady, measured beat was easy to tell apart from the ripping German guns across the line.",
    "m1911": "The .45 automatic was born in the Philippines, where American .38 revolvers had failed to stop charging Moro warriors, and Browning's answer was a heavy slug that settled matters at arm's length. Seven rounds, a grip safety and a hard kick: in trained hands it was a fine fighting pistol, in untrained ones it rarely hit much beyond a few yards. Colt, Remington Rand, Ithaca, Union Switch & Signal and even Singer, the sewing-machine firm, built them in the war.",
    "m97_trench": "Browning's exposed-hammer pump gun had no trigger disconnector, so a man could hold the trigger back and fire every time he worked the slide. With a perforated heat shield and a lug for the long M1917 bayonet, it went to France in 1918, and Germany protested formally that it broke the laws of war. Marines in the Pacific found it ideal for jungle trails and night perimeters, though paper-cased shells swelled in the damp until all-brass ones were issued.",
    # ------------------------------------------------------------------- UK
    "smle": "The 'Smelly' to the men who carried it: ten rounds in the magazine, filled from two chargers, and a bolt that cocked on closing and could be worked without lifting the cheek from the butt. Pre-1914 regulars trained to put fifteen aimed rounds into a target at 300 yards in a minute, and the legend grew that the Germans at Mons thought they faced machine guns. In this war it soldiered on with the Australians, whose Lithgow arsenal built it, and with the Indian Army, supplied from Ishapore.",
    "lee_no4": "The No. 4 was the old Lee-Enfield rebuilt for mass production: a heavier barrel poking out beyond the forestock, an aperture sight on the receiver, and fewer hand-fitted parts. Factories in Britain, Long Branch in Canada and Savage in the United States turned them out by the million, the American ones stamped 'U.S. PROPERTY'. Tommies were less fond of its little spike bayonet, the 'pigsticker', which could neither open a tin nor cut a tent peg.",
    "lee_no4t": "Rifles that shot best off the production line were set aside and sent to Holland & Holland, the London makers of big-game guns, to be fitted with the No. 32 telescope and a wooden cheekpiece on the butt. The scope travelled in its own steel transit case and went onto the rifle when the sniper went out. British snipers worked in pairs, one shooting while his mate watched through a long brass spotting telescope, and they were left largely to their own devices.",
    "sten": "Shepherd, Turpin and Enfield gave it its name; the men who carried it gave it others, from 'plumber's nightmare' to 'Stench gun'. Built from welded tube and stampings for about fifty shillings, it stripped in seconds and was dropped by the thousand to resistance fighters across occupied Europe. The single-feed magazine was its curse, so old hands loaded a couple of rounds short and never gripped it by the magazine; knocked or dropped, a Sten could fire by itself.",
    "owen": "It looked like nothing else - magazine sticking straight up, sights offset to the right - and Australian soldiers in New Guinea would not swap it for anything. Evelyn Owen, a young inventor from Wollongong, walled the bolt off from its cocking handle so mud and grit could not reach it, and in army trials it kept firing after being buried in mud and sand that stopped the Thompson and the Sten. Lysaght's works at Port Kembla built them, many painted in jungle camouflage.",
    "bren": "Brno plus Enfield: a Czech design reworked for the rimmed .303, and the best-loved weapon in the British section. The magazine held thirty, but the No. 2 loaded twenty-eight to spare the spring, and a hot barrel came out by its handle and a fresh one went in within seconds. It grouped so tightly that some gunners complained it was too accurate to spray an area, a fault most were happy to live with.",
    "vickers": "At High Wood in August 1916, ten Vickers guns of the Machine Gun Corps fired close to a million rounds in twelve hours, changing a hundred barrels and using every drop of water they could find. The jacket held some seven pints; steam ran down a hose into a can to condense and be poured back, so the gun could fire all day. In this war the machine-gun battalions of regiments like the Middlesex and the Cheshires still served it.",
    "webley": "Break the frame open and all six empties flew out together, which made the Webley quick to reload for a revolver. After the Great War the Army decided the old .455 kicked too hard for hastily trained officers and settled on the lighter .38/200 round. The official pattern was the near-identical Enfield No. 2, but Webley & Scott of Birmingham built their own in large numbers to fill the gap, and officers, tank crews and military policemen carried both.",
    "hipower": "Browning did not live to see it finished; Dieudonné Saive at FN completed it, and its thirteen-round magazine nearly doubled the Colt's seven. When Belgium fell the Germans kept FN building it for their own troops, so the Allies had John Inglis of Toronto make it instead. The first Inglis pistols went to Nationalist China with a tangent rear sight and a slot for a shoulder-stock holster; later ones went to Canadian and British troops, paratroopers and commandos among them.",
    "boys": "Code-named 'Stanchion' in development, it was renamed for its designer, Captain Boys, who died shortly before it was adopted. Over five feet long and thirty-six pounds in weight, with a monopod, a muzzle brake and a padded, sprung butt, it still kicked hard enough to leave a man sore for days. Against the light tanks of 1940 it served; by the desert fighting of 1941-42 it barely scratched what it met, and the PIAT replaced it.",
    # ----------------------------------------------------------------- USSR
    "mosin": "The trekhlineyka, the 'three-line rifle', took its name from its calibre in the old Russian measure of the line. Long, heavy and stiff in the bolt, it was sighted in with its spike bayonet fixed, and the Red Army left the bayonet there. Tula and Izhevsk turned them out in their millions, roughly finished in the war years, and they froze, rusted, went unoiled and kept working; the Finns captured so many that they reissued them to their own men.",
    "m44": "Adopted in 1944, the M44 was a short Mosin with its own cruciform bayonet hinged at the muzzle, folded back along the stock when not wanted and swung forward in a second. The short barrel threw out a great ball of flame and a deafening blast with every shot, and it kicked accordingly. It was handy in trenches, streets, tanks and the backs of lorries, and men who got one were glad to leave the long rifle behind.",
    "mosin_pu": "The PU was a small, simple scope, taken over from the abandoned SVT-40 sniper rifle and set on a side bracket above the action, with the bolt handle bent down to clear it. Selected rifles from Tula and Izhevsk were fitted and issued in great numbers, and it became the everyday rifle of the Soviet sniper. The Red Army press made heroes of its snipers at Stalingrad and after, and the Soviet Union trained women to the work in numbers no other army matched.",
    "svt40": "'Sveta' to the Red Army. Tokarev's rifle was light for a full-power semi-automatic, with a ten-round box and a muzzle brake that threw flame and dust at every shot. It needed its gas regulator set properly and its innards kept clean, which hurried conscripts in the mud of 1941 could not manage, while naval infantry and NCOs who cared for it swore by it. The Germans prized captured ones enough to issue them to their own men.",
    "ppsh": "Georgy Shpagin designed it to be stamped and welded by any factory with a press, with a chrome-lined barrel that shrugged off corrosive ammunition, and the Soviet Union turned out some six million. The 71-round drum had to be fitted to its own gun and took an age to fill, so men came to prefer the curved 35-round box that arrived in 1942. Whole companies carried nothing else, and German soldiers took every one they could find.",
    "ppd40": "Soviet planners had thought the submachine gun a weapon for border guards until the Winter War, when Finnish Suomi gunners taught them otherwise. Degtyaryov's gun was hurried back into production with a 71-round drum copied from the Suomi's. Beautifully machined, slow to make and expensive, it was soon pushed aside by the PPSh, which could be stamped out in a fraction of the time.",
    "pps43": "Alexei Sudayev designed it in besieged Leningrad, and the Sestroretsk works built the first ones there in 1942, sending them straight to the front a few miles away. It took far less steel and machine time than a PPSh, folded its stock flat over the receiver and fired automatic only, at a rate slow enough to keep on target. Tank crews, scouts and paratroopers liked it for its size, and some rate it the best submachine gun of the war.",
    "dp28": "Degtyaryov built it with remarkably few parts, and it ran through mud, frost and neglect. The flat 47-round pan on top turned as it fired, which earned it the name of 'record player', but the pans dented easily and were slow to refill. Its recoil spring sat under the barrel, where long bursts heated it until it went soft, and the bipod legs bent at a touch - faults put right in the DPM of 1944.",
    "maxim1910": "On its little two-wheeled Sokolov carriage with a steel shield, the Maxim weighed about as much as a man and was dragged along by its crew like a handcart, or set on skis in winter. It had fired from the tachanka horse-carts of the Civil War and would fire in the streets of Berlin. Wartime guns got a wide filler cap so crews could pack the jacket with snow, and it kept going long after cleverer guns had given up.",
    "tt33": "Tokarev took the Colt-Browning's swinging-link lock, simplified it, and made the hammer and lockwork lift out as one piece for cleaning. It fired the same hot, bottlenecked 7.62mm round as the PPSh, fast and flat-shooting, from an eight-round magazine. There was no safety catch beyond a half-cock notch, and officers carried it alongside the old Nagant revolver, which it never quite replaced.",
    "nagant1895": "As it was cocked, the Nagant pushed its cylinder forward so the mouth of the long cartridge case sealed the gap to the barrel. That trick made it one of the few revolvers that could take a silencer, and scouts and special units fitted it with the Bramit device. Reloading was misery: open a gate, push out each empty in turn with the rod, then feed in seven fresh rounds one at a time.",
    "ptrd": "When the Red Army called for anti-tank rifles in the summer of 1941, Degtyaryov had a working design within weeks, and his single-shot was the simpler of the two adopted. On firing, the barrel recoiled in the stock and the bolt unlocked and opened itself, flicking out the empty case for the loader to feed another. Nearly two metres long, it was carried by its two-man crew, and by 1943 it could not pierce a German tank from the front, so crews waited for tracks, sides, vision slits and the half-tracks behind.",
    "ptrs": "Simonov's answer to the same 1941 order was a gas-operated semi-automatic, loaded from below with five-round clips, that could fire far faster than Degtyaryov's gun. It was heavier, longer and more complicated, and came apart into two loads for its crew to carry on the march. Fewer were built, it wanted more care in mud and frost, and the Germans valued captured ones enough to use them.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------------- Germany
    "kar98k": "Called a carbine only because it was shorter than the Great War Gewehr 98, it was a full-power Mauser that kicked hard and shot straight. Some fourteen million were made, and in the later years they came with laminated stocks, stamped bands and a rougher finish. German doctrine built the section around its machine gun, and the riflemen with their 98ks spent much of their time carrying belts for it.",
    "kar98k_zf": "The German army went to war in 1939 with few snipers and paid for it on the Eastern Front against Soviet marksmen, and scoped 98ks were issued in growing numbers from 1942. They came with a bewildering variety of scopes and mounts - high turret, low turret, claw, side rail - each hand-fitted, so a scope belonged to its own rifle. Mountain troops such as the Austrian Matthäus Hetzenauer ran up the highest scores.",
    "g43": "Walther's rifle borrowed its gas system from the Soviet SVT-40s the Germans had been capturing since 1941, and it was far better than the clumsy G 41 before it. A detachable ten-round magazine and a rail for the small ZF 4 scope made it a favourite of snipers and marksmen. Renamed the K 43 in 1944, it was built in the hundreds of thousands, never enough to replace the bolt-action 98k.",
    "stg44": "Hugo Schmeisser at Haenel built it around a shortened cartridge, the 7.92mm Kurz, that could be fired in bursts from the shoulder yet still reach the middle distances where most infantry fighting happened. Hitler at first opposed it, so it went into production as the MP 43, a 'machine pistol'; by 1944 he had come round and named it 'Sturmgewehr', assault rifle. Over 400,000 were built, the first of them going to the Eastern Front.",
    "mp40": "The Allies called it the 'Schmeisser', though Hugo Schmeisser had almost nothing to do with it; it was an Erma gun, designed by Heinrich Vollmer. Its slow rate kept it on target, and with steel stampings, Bakelite and a folding stock it made a handy tool for section leaders, paratroopers and tank crews. Men learned not to grip it by the long magazine, which caused jams, and there was no handguard to keep a hot barrel off the hand.",
    "mp38": "A submachine gun with no wood about it at all - steel and Bakelite, with a folding stock - it was designed for paratroopers and vehicle crews and went through Poland and France with them. Its receiver was machined from solid steel and grooved along the side, slow and costly work. Early guns could fire if knocked with the bolt forward, so men strapped the cocking handle with a leather collar until a two-piece handle cured it, and the stamped MP 40 soon took its place on the production lines.",
    "mg34": "The first true general-purpose machine gun: on its bipod it was the section's light gun, on a tripod a heavy one, and in a tank turret it sat beside the main gun. A rocking trigger gave single shots from the top and bursts from the bottom, and a hot barrel slid out of the back when the receiver was twisted open. Beautifully machined, it was slow and costly to build and fussy in Russian mud and frost, which is why the MG 42 came after it.",
    "mg42": "Werner Gruner, a sheet-metal specialist from the Grossfuss metalware works, had to attend an army machine-gun course before he designed it, and the result was stamped, riveted and quick to build. It fired so fast that single shots blurred into one tearing burst; Americans called it 'Hitler's buzzsaw', and the British called every German machine gun a 'Spandau'. A worn barrel could be changed in seconds, and crews carried spares because it ate them.",
    "mg42_tripod": "On the Lafette tripod the MG 42 became a heavy machine gun, with a buffered cradle, fine traverse and elevation, and a periscopic sight the gunner could use from behind cover. A mechanism in the cradle could sweep the fire up and down through a band of range by itself, walking a beaten zone across the ground. The tripod folded onto a man's back like a pack frame, and at night the gun could be laid on fixed lines to cover a road or a gap in the wire.",
    "mg34_tripod": "On its Lafette the MG 34 was the heavy machine gun of the battalion's machine-gun company, with an optical sight and heavy-bulleted sS ammunition for long-range and indirect fire. A well-sited crew could fire over the heads of their own advancing infantry and drop plunging fire onto the far side of a hill. It went through Poland, France and Russia this way, and served on beside the MG 42 to the end.",
    "p38": "Walther's pistol was meant to replace the Luger at lower cost, and it did something few army pistols could: carried loaded with the hammer down, it fired its first shot with one long double-action pull. A little pin stuck out of the back of the slide when a round was chambered, so a man could feel it in the dark. Walther, Mauser and Spreewerk all built it, and Allied soldiers counted one a fine souvenir, if not quite a Luger.",
    "p08": "Georg Luger's toggle rose like a knee at every shot, and the sharply raked grip made the pistol point as naturally as a finger. It was beautifully made, expensive and touchy about mud, and its cartridge, the 9mm Parabellum, outlived it to become the world's pistol round. Army production ended in 1942, but officers and NCOs carried it to the end, and every GI and Tommy wanted to bring one home.",
    "pzb39": "A light single-shot anti-tank rifle with a falling-block breech, it fired a small 7.92mm bullet from a huge case at tremendous speed. The bullet carried a pellet of tear gas meant to drive a crew out through their hatches; it seldom did. Ammunition boxes clamped to the sides of the action held ready rounds, but by 1941 it could no longer beat the armour it met, and many were cut down into the GrB 39 grenade launcher.",
    "fg42": "Crete in 1941 taught the German paratroopers a hard lesson: they jumped with pistols and grenades while their rifles and machine guns came down separately in canisters, often among the enemy. The Luftwaffe wanted one weapon to do the work of rifle, submachine gun and light machine gun, and Louis Stange of Rheinmetall gave them the FG 42, with a side-mounted magazine, a bipod and a built-in spike bayonet. Only a few thousand were ever made, and paratroopers carried it on the Gran Sasso raid of 1943.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- Italy
    "carcano": "Italy tried to move to a harder-hitting 7.35mm round in 1938, then went to war with factories and depots still full of the old 6.5mm, and most short rifles ended up back in the old calibre. Six rounds came in an en-bloc clip that dropped out of the bottom of the magazine when the last was fired. Light and handy, with a fixed rear sight set for a single battle range, it went with Italian soldiers to North Africa, Greece and the Don.",
    "mab38": "Tullio Marengoni of Beretta built it like a sporting gun: walnut stock, blued steel, a slotted compensator and two triggers, the front for single shots and the rear for bursts. It was heavier and better finished than it needed to be, and soldiers on every side wanted one. German troops prized it, and after the Italian armistice of 1943 they kept the Beretta works making it for their own use.",
    "breda30": "Its magazine was fixed to the right side of the gun and swung forward to be filled from twenty-round chargers, so a bent magazine put the whole weapon out of action. An oil pump lubricated every round on the way in, and in the Libyan desert the oiled cartridges gathered sand until the gun choked. The barrel changed quickly but had no handle, so gunners burnt their hands, and few Italian soldiers had a kind word for it.",
    "breda37": "Heavy and slow-firing, the Breda M37 was trusted by the Italian soldier far more than the Breda light gun. It fed from rigid twenty-round trays, and its strangest habit was to put each fired case back into the tray, so the loader had to pick out the empties before refilling it. Set on its tripod it served in North Africa, the Balkans and Russia, and the Germans later took it over too.",
    "beretta34": "Small, solid and almost impossible to wear out, the M1934 had the open-topped slide that marked Beretta pistols for decades. When the magazine ran dry the follower held the slide open, and it slammed shut the moment the magazine was withdrawn, an odd arrangement. It fired the modest 9mm Corto round, and Allied soldiers in North Africa and Italy picked them up by the thousand as souvenirs.",
    "solothurn": "The S-18/1100 was an enormous rifle firing a 20mm cannon shell from a ten-round magazine on its left side, and it could even fire automatic. Over two metres long and weighing more than forty kilograms, it was broken down for its crew to carry or towed on a little two-wheeled carriage. Italy used it in North Africa and Hungary adopted it as the 36M, and by 1942 it too was outmatched by the armour it was meant to stop.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- Japan
    "type38": "With its long bayonet fixed, the Type 38 stood taller than many of the men who carried it. Its 6.5mm round kicked mildly and left little flash or smoke, and its action was one of the strongest of its day. The imperial chrysanthemum stamped on the receiver marked it as the Emperor's property, and many were ground off before rifles were surrendered in 1945; its rattling sliding dust cover was often left in camp.",
    "type99": "The Type 99 brought a heavier 7.7mm round and a shorter barrel, and early rifles came with a folding wire monopod and a rear sight with hinged arms for leading aircraft, which spoke more of hope than of practice. The bore was chrome-lined against jungle rust. As the war turned, the extras vanished; late 'last-ditch' rifles had crude fixed sights, wooden buttplates and rough stocks, though the chrysanthemum still went on the receiver.",
    "type97_sniper": "Selected Type 38s became the Type 97, with a bent-down bolt handle, a wire monopod and a low-powered scope set off to the left so the rifle could still be loaded from chargers. The 6.5mm round fired from its long barrel gave so little flash and noise that Americans and Australians found it maddening to tell where the shots came from. Japanese snipers tied themselves into palm tops or lay in spider holes, content to wait for hours.",
    "type100": "Japan came late to the submachine gun, and the Type 100 was built only in small numbers, firing the weak 8mm Nambu pistol round from a curved magazine on the left side. True to the army's habits, it carried a lug for the standard rifle bayonet. A simplified version with a faster rate appeared in 1944, and some went to paratroopers with a folding stock, but most Japanese infantry never saw one.",
    "type11": "Kijiro Nambu's Type 11 fed from a hopper on its left side, filled with six of the rifleman's own five-round chargers, so any soldier could keep the gun supplied. The catch was an oiler that greased every round, and in dust or sand the oiled cartridges picked up grit until the gun jammed. It needed a reduced-charge cartridge to run well, fired only automatic and had an oddly offset stock, but it served from China to the Pacific islands.",
    "type96": "The Type 96 looked a good deal like the Czech ZB, with a curved thirty-round magazine on top and a quick-change barrel with a carrying handle. The army expected its gunners to charge with it, and gave it a lug for the rifleman's bayonet. Many were fitted with a low-power telescopic sight, and in the Pacific Allied soldiers learned to fear its steady, accurate fire from well-hidden positions.",
    "type99_lmg": "When the army moved to the 7.7mm round, the Type 96 was redesigned to take it, and the Type 99 was the result: tougher, better made, and with a monopod under the butt for steady fire from a fixed position. It kept the bayonet lug and the option of a telescopic sight. Production never caught up with demand, and many units fought on with the older 6.5mm guns to the end.",
    "type92": "Americans called it the 'woodpecker' for the slow, knocking rhythm of its fire, which made it easy to pick out across the jungle. A heavy Hotchkiss-type gun in 7.7mm, it fed from thirty-round metal strips and sat on a tripod with sockets front and back for carrying poles, so a crew could lift the whole thing and move it like a stretcher. It was accurate at long range, often fitted with an optical sight, and dug in on the islands it was deadly.",
    "nambu14": "From a distance it looked like a Luger; inside it was nothing like one. The Type 14 fired the weak 8mm Nambu round from an eight-round magazine, and its striker spring was prone to weaken and misfire. Late examples got a huge trigger guard so a gloved finger would fit, a lesson of the Manchurian winter, and American soldiers carried them home as souvenirs in great numbers.",
    "type97_at": "Near sixty kilos of gas-operated 20mm rifle, it came with carrying handles front and rear so four men could lift it like a stretcher, and an optional steel shield. The recoil was violent even with the muzzle brake, and the gun slammed back in its cradle at every shot. Against light tanks it could do real harm, but by the time Shermans came ashore on the islands it could do little but harass them.",
    # --------------------------------------------------------------- France
    "mas36": "Designed after the Great War around the new rimless 7.5mm cartridge, the MAS-36 was short, stout and plain, with an aperture rear sight and a bolt handle bent forward that looked awkward. It had no safety catch at all; the soldier simply carried it with the chamber empty. A cruciform spike bayonet lived reversed in a tube under the barrel, and in May 1940 too few had been issued, so most French infantry fought with older rifles.",
    "lebel": "In 1886 the Lebel was the first rifle built for smokeless powder, and for a few years it made every other army's rifle look old. Eight rounds went one at a time into a tube under the barrel, and refilling it under fire was a slow business. The poilus of 1914-18 fixed its long needle bayonet, 'Rosalie', and in 1940 reservists and colonial troops still carried it into battle.",
    "mas38": "Compact and cleverly made, the MAS-38 had its barrel angled slightly to the receiver so the bolt could run back into a tube inside the butt, and pushing the trigger forward locked the bolt as a safety. It fired the weak 7.65mm Longue round that no other army used. Few had reached French troops by June 1940; the Germans took the rest, and others later found their way to the Resistance.",
    "fm2429": "The first version of 1924 burst barrels, partly because a German 8mm round would chamber in it, so the cartridge was shortened in 1929 and the trouble ended. After that it was a good gun, with a top-mounted 25-round magazine and two triggers, the front for single shots and the rear for bursts. It had no quick-change barrel, so gunners kept their bursts short; France kept it in service long after the war.",
    "hotchkiss1914": "The Hotchkiss had been the French army's machine gun in the Great War: heavy, air-cooled, with big cooling rings on the barrel and a gas system that almost never failed. It fed from rigid metal strips, which the loader held level as they slid through the gun, or from articulated belts of short strips. The American Expeditionary Force used it in 1918, and in 1940 French machine-gunners still set it up on the same tripod.",
    "mas1935": "The Swiss engineer Charles Petter designed it for SACM, and it was neat, slim and well made, with the hammer and lockwork in a unit that lifted out for cleaning. Its weak point was the 7.65mm Longue round, too feeble for a service pistol. Petter's patents later passed to SIG in Switzerland, where the design grew into one of the finest pistols ever made.",
    # --------------------------------------------------------------- Poland
    "wz29": "The Polish soldier's rifle in September 1939 was this short Mauser, built at the state arsenal at Radom, close kin to the Czech vz. 24 and firing the same 7.92mm round. It had a turned-down bolt handle and was well made and dependable. It fought on the Bzura and in Warsaw, and so many were captured that the Germans issued them to their own second-line troops.",
    "wz28": "Poland took the Browning automatic rifle from FN in Belgium and built its own version in 7.92mm at the state rifle factory in Warsaw, with a pistol grip and a bipod. With its twenty-round magazine it was the section's light machine gun in September 1939, respected for its reliability if not its capacity. Its crews had to fire in short bursts and change magazines often, and the gun asked little else of them.",
    "ckm_wz30": "Poland took the water-cooled Browning M1917, reworked it for the 7.92mm Mauser round and built it at home as the ckm wz. 30, the army's standard heavy machine gun. Fed from 250-round cloth belts, it could fire for long spells without overheating, and it also armed Poland's 7TP light tanks. In September 1939 its crews held river lines and villages against heavy odds.",
    "vis": "Piotr Wilniewczyc and Jan Skrzypiński designed it and Radom built it: a heavy 9mm pistol that soaked up recoil, with a Polish eagle on the slide. It had no safety catch in the usual sense, only a lever that dropped the hammer safely. Under occupation the Germans kept Radom making it as the P 35(p), without the eagle, while Polish workers smuggled out parts for the underground.",
    "ur_wz35": "Its code name, 'Ur', was said to stand for Uruguay, the supposed destination of the sealed crates in which it was stored. The rifle fired a small bullet so fast that it could punch through the thin plate of German light tanks, and it was light enough for one man to carry. Troops only saw and trained with it when war was almost upon them in 1939, and the Germans and Italians later used captured ones.",
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- China
    "hanyang88": "Hanyang Arsenal on the Yangtze had copied Germany's 1888 Commission rifle since the 1890s and was still making it in the 1940s, around a million in all. Its five rounds came in a clip that dropped out through the bottom of the magazine when empty. By 1937 many were old and worn, but warlord armies, Nationalist divisions and Communist guerrillas all carried it, and it was the most common Chinese rifle of the war.",
    "chiang": "Zhongzheng was the formal given name of Chiang Kai-shek, and the rifle that bore it was a copy of the Mauser Standardmodell, the best rifle China could build. Production began in 1935, and when the Japanese advanced, the arsenals were packed up and carried west up the Yangtze to go on working. The German-trained Nationalist divisions that fought at Shanghai in 1937 carried Mausers of this pattern.",
    "zb26": "Václav Holek's gun from Brno was simple, light and very hard to stop, with a top-mounted magazine and a barrel that came off in seconds; the British liked it enough to make it the Bren. China bought it in large numbers and copied it, and Chinese soldiers called it simply the 'Czech-style' gun. The Germans, after taking Czechoslovakia, issued it to their own troops.",
    "c96": "The Chinese called it the 'box cannon' for its wooden holster, which clipped to the grip as a shoulder stock. A 1919 arms embargo kept military rifles out of China but said nothing of pistols, so warlords bought the Mauser by the shipload, and Chinese arsenals copied it. The select-fire versions emptied a magazine in the blink of an eye and climbed off target just as fast, but officers, bodyguards and guerrillas prized them.",
    "type24_maxim": "The Type 24 was a Chinese copy of the German MG 08 Maxim, built at the Jinling Arsenal in Nanjing from 1935 and named for the 24th year of the Republic. Water-cooled, belt-fed and very heavy, it was the Nationalist army's standard heavy machine gun, usually mounted on a tripod rather than the German sledge. Its crews fought it at Shanghai in 1937 and on through the long years of the war.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------------- Finland
    "m39": "The Finns called it 'Ukko-Pekka', after the nickname of their old president Svinhufvud. It was built on Mosin actions, many of them captured Russian, with a heavier barrel, better sights, an improved trigger and a stock with a pistol grip. It shot far tighter than any Russian rifle had a right to, and Finnish soldiers regarded it as the best of their Mosins.",
    "suomi": "Aimo Lahti's gun was machined from solid steel, heavy as a rifle and accurate far beyond what a pistol round deserved. In the Winter War, Finnish ski troops with Suomis ambushed Soviet columns stalled on forest roads, and the Red Army hurried its own submachine guns back into production with a drum copied from it. The 71-round drum was heavy, and many preferred the slimmer four-column 50-round box, known for its shape as the 'coffin'.",
    "lahti_saloranta": "Aimo Lahti and Arvo Saloranta built a light machine gun that was accurate and well made but complicated, heavy for its role and sensitive to dirt. The Finns carried it through the Winter War, but given the chance a Finnish section swapped it for a captured Soviet DP, which they nicknamed 'Emma'. The DP was cruder, but it kept firing in conditions that stopped the Lahti-Saloranta.",
    "maxim_m32": "Old Russian Maxims were rebuilt by the Finns to their own standard, and the M/32-33 fired faster than the Soviet original. Its wide filler cap let the crew pack the water jacket with snow and ice, a trick the Red Army soon copied. In the Winter War these guns, dug in along the forest edges, cut down Soviet infantry crossing open snow and frozen lakes.",
    "lahti_l35": "Aimo Lahti designed his pistol for a Finnish winter, sealing it against snow and grit and fitting a bolt accelerator that helped drive the action through its cycle when cold thickened the oil. It was heavy, well made, soft to shoot and utterly dependable, but also slow and expensive to build, so Finland never had enough. Sweden built it under licence as the m/40.",
    "lahti_l39": "'Norsupyssy', the Finns called it, the elephant gun: a 20mm semi-automatic rifle of nearly fifty kilos, fed from a ten-round magazine and hauled about by its crew. Its bipod could take ski-like runners for snow. By 1943 it could not stop a T-34 from the front, so crews turned it on light armour, bunker slits and men at long range.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------------- Hungary
    "35m": "Hungary's 35M was a turn-bolt Mannlicher, loaded with a five-round en-bloc clip that dropped out of the bottom when empty, and it fired the rimmed 8mm Hungarian round. It had a two-piece stock and was well made at the Budapest arms factory. The Germans liked it enough to order it in 7.92mm with a Mauser magazine, and Hungarian soldiers carried it to the Don in 1942.",
    "kiraly": "Pál Király's gun looked more like a carbine than a submachine gun, with a long barrel, a full wooden stock and a bayonet lug, and it fired the powerful 9mm Mauser Export round. Its long magazine folded forward into a slot in the stock for carrying. Hungarian troops fought with it on the Eastern Front and through the siege of Budapest, and many rate it among the best submachine guns of the war.",
    "solothurn31m": "Designed in Germany, built by Solothurn in Switzerland and Steyr in Austria to get round the Versailles treaty, and adopted by Austria and Hungary, the 31M was an ancestor of the MG 34. It fed from a curved box magazine on the left side, and its rocking trigger, top for single shots and bottom for bursts, reappeared on its German descendant. Well made and accurate, it served the Hungarian army throughout the war.",
    "schwarzlose": "Andreas Schwarzlose's gun had no locked breech at all, held shut by a heavy bolt, a toggle and a stout spring, and it was simple, rugged and cheap. That called for a short barrel, which threw a great fireball at the muzzle, so the Hungarian conversion of 1931 for the pointed 8mm round added a long conical flash hider. The Austro-Hungarian army had fought with it through the Great War, and Hungary kept it as a standard heavy gun in this one.",
    "frommer37m": "Rudolf Frommer's 37M was simple, sturdy and handsome: a blowback pistol in 9mm Short with a grip safety, made at the Budapest arms factory. The Germans ordered a version in 7.65mm, with an added safety catch, for the Luftwaffe. Hungarian officers carried it throughout the war, and it took hard use well.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------------- Romania
    "vz24": "Brno's version of the Mauser was shorter and handier than the Great War Gewehr 98, very well made, and it sold all over the world. Romania bought it in huge numbers as its standard rifle, and China was another large customer. After 1939 the Germans took over Czech production, and Romanian soldiers carried the vz. 24 first against the Soviets and, after August 1944, against the Germans.",
    "orita": "Designed by the Czech engineer Leopold Jaška and built at the Cugir arsenal, the Orița had a long barrel and a tangent rear sight graduated to ranges no submachine gun had any business reaching. It was well made, accurate and dependable, and Romanian troops carried it on the Eastern Front from 1942. A later model with a folding stock followed.",
    # ------------------------------------------------------ AT launchers
    "bazooka": "It took its name from the homemade horn of the radio comedian Bob Burns. Two men worked it: the loader slid a rocket into the back, wired it to the contact and tapped the gunner's helmet, and the gunner fired it electrically. Captured examples in Tunisia helped the Germans build the Panzerschreck, and by 1944 American crews knew its small warhead needed a side or rear shot to stop a Panther.",
    "panzerschreck": "The Germans enlarged the bazookas they captured in Tunisia into an 88mm weapon, and the men called it 'Ofenrohr', stovepipe, for its shape and the smoke that poured from it. The first model's rocket was still burning as it left the tube, so the gunner wore a gas mask and gloves; the RPzB 54 added a steel shield with a small window. It fired from a magneto rather than batteries, which kept it working in frost, and its backblast was deadly to anyone behind.",
    "piat": "Projector, Infantry, Anti-Tank: a spigot launcher driven by a spring so strong that cocking it meant standing on the shoulder pad and hauling the body up with your back. Recoil was meant to re-cock it for the next shot, and often didn't. It had no backblast, so it could be fired from inside a room, and Major Robert Cain at Arnhem and Private Smokey Smith in Italy both won the Victoria Cross with one.",
    "pzf30k": "The first Panzerfaust, built by HASAG in Leipzig, was a steel tube with a small hollow-charge head, held under the arm and aimed over a flip-up sight. The propellant blew its gas out of the back, so there was no recoil, but the range was so short that the firer had to let a tank come almost on top of him. Its small, pointed head could glance off sloped armour, and the bigger-headed Panzerfaust 30 soon followed.",
    "pzf60": "The Panzerfaust 60 was the most common of them all: a longer-ranged tube with the big hollow-charge head, simple enough for a Volkssturm boy or grandfather to use after a few minutes' instruction. 'Achtung! Feuerstrahl!' - 'Caution! Jet of flame!' - was printed on the tube to warn of the backblast. In Berlin, Soviet tank crews hung bedsprings and wire mesh on their tanks to set off its warhead before it struck the armour.",
    "pzf100": "Arriving late in 1944, the Panzerfaust 100 used a two-stage propellant charge to throw the same big warhead out to a hundred metres, with a sight marked for the longer shots. It was heavier than the 60, and fewer were made before the end. Soviet and Allied tank crews met it in the last months of the war in the streets of German towns, and the idea of a cheap, throwaway tank-killer outlived the war.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------- flamethrowers
    "m2_flamer": "Seventy pounds of fuel and pressure tanks on a man's back, the M2-2 threw a rod of napalm-thickened fire that bent into bunker slits and cave mouths, lit by a revolving cylinder of igniter cartridges. The fuel lasted only a few seconds, and every Japanese rifleman in sight aimed at the man carrying it. On Iwo Jima Corporal Hershel 'Woody' Williams won the Medal of Honor clearing pillboxes with one.",
    "flammenwerfer41": "The assault pioneers carried it against bunkers, pillboxes and fortified houses: two tanks side by side, one of fuel and one of pressurising gas, and a flame gun that threw fire some twenty-five metres. Its hydrogen igniter failed in the Russian winter and was replaced with one that used cartridges. The man carrying it drew every gun in range, and the crews went in close behind smoke and covering fire.",
    "roks2": "Its fuel tank was shaped like an ordinary infantry pack and its flame gun like a rifle, so that enemy snipers would not single out the man carrying it. Blank cartridges lit each burst, and the simpler ROKS-3 followed in 1942. Soviet flamethrower companies and the assault engineer brigades used them in street fighting from Stalingrad to Berlin, often wearing steel breastplates.",
    "type93_flamer": "The Type 93 had three tanks - two for fuel and one for pressurising gas - and a flame gun lit by a revolving cylinder of cartridges that was not always reliable. Japanese engineers used it against pillboxes and fortified villages in China and in the early Pacific campaigns, and an improved Type 100 replaced it from 1940. It was heavy, short-ranged and dangerous to carry.",
    "lanciafiamme": "Italy had used flamethrowers against the Austrians in the Great War, and the M35 was a man-pack model for the engineers, with its fuel and a compressed gas bottle carried on the back. In North Africa the guastatori, Italy's assault engineers, turned flamethrowers on wire, bunkers and dug-in positions. Like every flamethrower, it made its carrier a marked man.",
    # -------------------------------------------------------------- mortars
    "m2_mortar": "Built under licence from a French Brandt design, the 60mm M2 was the rifle company's own artillery: light enough for its crew to keep up with the riflemen, with a baseplate, a bipod and a simple sight. It threw high explosive, smoke and illuminating shells, and in the Pacific the flares were valued as highly as the HE for lighting up the wire in front of a perimeter at night. A good crew could have several bombs in the air before the first one landed.",
    "2in_mortar": "The platoon commander's own artillery, carried by a two-man team in platoon headquarters: a short tube on a small spade baseplate, with no bipod, fired by a trigger. Early ones had no proper sight, only a white line painted down the barrel to aim by, and the crew judged range by eye and experience. Its high explosive was light, but its smoke bombs were invaluable for screening a section's dash across open ground.",
    "50rm38": "The 50-RM 38 was set at one of two fixed angles, and range was changed by a valve that let part of the propellant gas escape from the tube. It was too complicated, too heavy for its small bomb and too slow to bring into action, and it was simplified in 1940 and again in 1941. By 1943 the Red Army had decided the 50mm company mortar was not worth having and gave the job to the 82mm.",
    "legrw36": "Rheinmetall's little mortar was a precision instrument: a trigger-fired tube on a baseplate with fine traverse and elevation gear and, at first, a telescopic sight that was a small marvel in itself. It was also heavy for what it did, and its small bomb burst with little effect in soft ground. Production stopped in 1941, and by 1943 it had been withdrawn from the front line.",
    "type89": "Americans called it the 'knee mortar' for its curved baseplate, and some who assumed it was fired braced on the thigh broke their legs finding out otherwise. It was really a rifled, trigger-fired tube held at about forty-five degrees on the ground, with range set by screwing the firing pin up or down the barrel. It threw a 50mm shell or an ordinary hand grenade with a propellant charge attached, and it was one of the most effective weapons the Japanese infantry had.",
    "brixia": "Brixia is the old Latin name for Brescia, where it was made, and the M35 was one of the most complicated light mortars ever built: breech-loaded, trigger-fired, with a gas valve to set range. It folded into a pack for its carrier, and in action the gunner rested on its padded frame. It could fire very quickly and quite accurately, but its tiny 45mm bomb burst with little effect.",
    "wz36": "The granatnik wz. 36 was a light Polish mortar held at a fixed angle, its range set by a valve venting gas from the barrel. Light enough for one man to carry, it gave the Polish rifle platoon its own high-explosive support in 1939. Its bombs were small but its crews used it well, and the Germans put captured ones into service.",
    "grw34": "The 8 cm GrW 34 broke down into three loads - tube, bipod and baseplate - and a drilled crew could set it up and have bombs in the air in moments. Its reputation for uncanny accuracy owed more to those crews and their careful fire plans than to any secret of design. In Normandy, Allied infantry came to dread German mortars more than almost anything else, and a large share of the casualties in the hedgerows came from them.",
    "82bm37": "The 82mm was the Red Army's workhorse mortar, with a round baseplate that could be turned any way and springs between tube and bipod to soak up the shock of firing. At a pinch it could fire captured German 81mm bombs, though the German tube would not take Soviet ones. After 1943 it took over the company mortar's job too, and it was carried from Stalingrad to Berlin.",
    "m1_81mm": "The battalion's heavy mortar broke into three loads - tube, bipod and baseplate - and was often trundled on a handcart or carried in a jeep. It fired light and heavy high explosive, smoke and white phosphorus, the 'Willie Peter' that burned through almost anything and screened an advance at the same time. Its heavy shell hit harder than anything else the infantry battalion owned, and a good crew could put rounds where the rifle companies needed them within minutes of a call.",
    "3in_mortar": "Carried in Universal Carriers by the battalion's mortar platoon, the 3-inch was set up behind the rifle companies as their own artillery. In the early years it was badly outranged by the German 8 cm, until improved propellant charges from 1942 gave it the reach it needed. Its bomb was heavy and its crews well drilled, and from the desert to North-West Europe it was the first fire a battalion could call on.",
    "type97_81": "A sound Brandt-pattern 81mm mortar, heavy but accurate, the Type 97 was used by the Japanese with great skill. Dug into ridges and caves with every stretch of beach and ravine registered in advance, as on Iwo Jima and Okinawa, Japanese mortars caused terrible losses among the men coming ashore. A lighter, shorter Type 99 was also made for the infantry.",
    "brandt27": "The Stokes mortar of the Great War threw a plain cylinder that tumbled in flight; Edgar Brandt gave it a streamlined, finned bomb and a proper bipod with fine adjustment, and the modern mortar was born. The Mle 27/31 was licensed or copied almost everywhere: the American M1, the Japanese Type 97 and the Italian 81mm all came from it. France, Poland and China fought with it.",
    "m81_14": "The '81/14' in its name gives the calibre and a barrel fourteen calibres long. It was a good Brandt-type mortar that fired both a light and a heavy bomb, the light one to longer ranges than most of its rivals could reach. Italian crews carried it in North Africa and Russia, and it was among the better weapons Italy had.",
}
